PART 6: BEST PRACTICES

================================================================================

Best Practice 1: Keep Expressions Simple

Good:

{price * quantity}


Harder to read:

{price * quantity * tax * (1 + discount) - shipping + handling}


Simple expressions are generally:

Easier to read.
Easier to debug.
Easier to maintain.

The main benefit here is readability and maintainability, rather than automatically better runtime performance.

================================================================================

Best Practice 2: Extract Complex Logic

================================================================================

Instead of placing large calculations directly in JSX:

const getTotalPrice = () => {
  return price * quantity * (1 + tax);
};

return (
  <p>
    Total: ${getTotalPrice()}
  </p>
);


This separates the calculation from the rendered markup.

Complex Logic
     ↓
Function / Variable
     ↓
Simple JSX


Benefits:

Benefit
------------------------------
Improved readability
Easier testing
Easier debugging
Easier maintenance
Potential reuse


================================================================================

Best Practice 3: Use Meaningful Variable Names

================================================================================

Good
const bookTitle =
  'The Great Gatsby';

const authorName =
  'F. Scott Fitzgerald';

return (
  <div>

    <h1>{bookTitle}</h1>

    <p>
      By {authorName}
    </p>

  </div>
);

Avoid
const t = 'The Great Gatsby';
const a = 'F. Scott Fitzgerald';

return (
  <div>

    <h1>{t}</h1>

    <p>By {a}</p>

  </div>
);


Comparison:

Unclear   | Better Name
----------|----------------
t         | bookTitle
a         | authorName


Meaningful names make code easier to understand and maintain.

================================================================================

Best Practice 4: Use Stable Keys in Lists

================================================================================

Preferred when each object has a unique ID:

{books.map(book => (

  <li key={book.id}>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}


Avoid relying on an array index when list order/content can change:

{books.map((book, index) => (

  <li key={index}>
    {book.title}
  </li>

))}

Why?
Stable ID                  | Array Index
---------------------------|----------------------------------
Belongs to the data item   | Represents current position
Stable after reordering    | Can change after reordering
Better identity tracking   | Can cause incorrect identity reuse


